<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valdemar IV of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1320–1375</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Valdemar Atterdag</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valdemar IV, known as Valdemar Atterdag, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from 1340 to 1375 and rebuilt the kingdom after years in which its provinces had been pawned to foreign lords, recovering Zealand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> and other lands. In 1361 he invaded Gotland and took Visby, provoking war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, which forced him to accept the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> in 1370. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a> later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Valdemar IV of Denmark" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown contemporary 14th-century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waldemar_IV_Otherday_of_Denmark_c_1375_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Valdemar was the youngest son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-ii-of-denmark">Christopher II of Denmark</a> and Euphemia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. After his father's defeats and the death and imprisonment of his older brothers at the hands of the Holsteiners, he spent his youth in exile at the court of Emperor Louis IV in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, acting as a pretender. After Niels Ebbesen and his warriors assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-iii-count-of-holstein-rendsburg">Gerhard III, Count of Holstein-Rendsburg</a>, Valdemar was proclaimed king at the Viborg Assembly on 24 June 1340. Through his marriage to Helvig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> he controlled about a quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> north of the Kongeå.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under Christopher II Denmark had gone bankrupt and been mortgaged out in parcels. Valdemar paid off the mortgage on the rest of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> with taxes from peasants above the Kongeå, and in 1344 he recovered North Friesland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> gave him Copenhagen Castle and town, and he was the first Danish king to rule from Copenhagen. For 19,000 marks he gave up Danish Estonia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, to pay mortgages on more important parts of Denmark. In 1346 he took back Vordingborg Castle, headquarters of the Holsteiners, and by 1347 he had thrown out the Germans.</p><p>In 1349 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> arrived, and general figures for 1349 to 1350 range between 33 and 66 percent of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Valdemar refused to reduce taxes the following year though fewer peasants farmed less land, and uprisings flared in the following years. In 1360 he forced King Magnus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> and for all intents regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, also capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>.</p><p>In 1361 Valdemar invaded Gotland and defeated the Gotlanders in front of Visby, killing 1800 men. Visby belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and in 1362 the Hansa states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> allied against him. They captured and pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and, with rebellious nobles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, forced him out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> in 1368. He signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> in 1370, acknowledging the Hansa's rights in the herring trade and its tax exemptions, and returned to Denmark after four years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Valdemar died at Gurre Castle in North Zealand on 24 October 1375 and was buried at Sorø Abbey. His heavy taxation and his usurpation of rights long held by noble families led to uprisings throughout his reign, and the great landed families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> opposed him bitterly. He expanded royal power on the basis of his military prowess and a loyal nobility that became the foundation of Danish rulers until 1440. His grandson Olaf II, son of his daughter Margaret and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vi-of-norway">Haakon VI of Norway</a>, was elected as his successor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valdemar IV of Denmark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great test came when <b>Valdemar IV of Denmark</b> sacked Visby in 1361 and defeated a Hanseatic fleet at Helsingborg in 1362.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Valdemar IV Atterdag</b> sacked Visby on Gotland in 1361 but lost the ensuing war with the Hanseatic League, ended by the Treaty of Stralsund in 1370.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The St George's Night Uprising of 1343 persuaded the Danish crown that its Estonian lands were too costly to hold, and in 1346 <b>Valdemar IV</b> sold them to the Teutonic Order, which entrusted them to its Livonian branch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the north, German trading towns led by Lübeck formed the Hanseatic League, whose victory over <b>Valdemar IV of Denmark</b>, sealed by the Treaty of Stralsund in 1370, marked the height of its power.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Livonian Order crushed the revolt, and in 1346 <b>Valdemar IV</b> sold the duchy to the Teutonic Order, which handed it to its Livonian branch.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-estonia-1219-1346">Duchy of Estonia (1219-1346)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1361 <b>Valdemar IV of Denmark</b> invaded, massacring the farmers' army outside Visby.</blockquote><a href="/state/gotland">Gotland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tikøb, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Gurre Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.02,12.45&amp;year=1355" aria-label="Tikøb on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 256 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Valdemar IV of Denmark"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 526h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 526h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.02,12.45&amp;year=1355">What was here in 1355? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
